## 2. State and Updates

### 2.1 Observable Models Drive Views

**CRITICAL:** Read `@Observable` model properties inside tracked methods; UIKit invalidates and re-runs them on change.

❌ Bad: `var name: String { didSet { label.text = name } }` (manual sync in every setter)
✅ Good: `override func updateProperties() { super.updateProperties(); label.text = model.name }`
⚠️ Why: Manual sync drifts, duplicates code, misses updates
🔧 Fix: iOS 26+: `updateProperties()`; iOS 18–25: `viewWillLayoutSubviews` / `layoutSubviews` with plist key; call `setNeedsUpdateProperties()` only for non-observable inputs
📍 See: <https://developer.apple.com/documentation/uikit/updating-views-automatically-with-observation-tracking-in-uikit>

**Detect:** `didSet { someView.` patterns, `NotificationCenter`/Combine used only to refresh labels, `ObservableObject` + `sink` in new UIKit code, calling `updateProperties()` directly

### 2.2 Content in updateProperties, Geometry in Layout

❌ Bad: `override func layoutSubviews() { super.layoutSubviews(); label.text = model.title; badge.isHidden = model.count == 0 }`
✅ Good: Text/colors/visibility in `updateProperties()`; frames/constraints in `layoutSubviews()` / `updateConstraints()`
⚠️ Why: Content changes then force full layout passes; slower and harder to reason about
🔧 Fix: Split content from geometry; use `.flushUpdates` animation option instead of manual `layoutIfNeeded()` around state changes (iOS 26+)

**Detect:** Text or color assignments in `layoutSubviews`, `layoutIfNeeded()` inside animation blocks after model mutation

